From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philip Rogers <pdr@chromium.org>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:56:32 -0700
Subject: Repeat lifecycle after ResizeObserver loop limit error event

When a ResizeObserver reaches its delivery depth limit, we fire a loop
limit error event. An onerror handler can run arbitrary script that
invalidates another frame (e.g., an ancestor) while leaving its own
frame clean, or invalidates layout or paint properties (e.g., text data
or scroll offsets) without lowering DocumentLifecycle below
kPrePaintClean. LocalFrameView::NotifyResizeObservers() previously
returned false in these cases because it only checked whether its own
DocumentLifecycle state remained >= kPrePaintClean.

Return true from LocalFrameView::NotifyResizeObservers() whenever the
loop limit error event is dispatched so that the lifecycle loop repeats
to clean up any state dirtied by script. Because
SetLoopLimitErrorDispatched(true) is set before returning, this repeats
at most once per frame per lifecycle update. Also always call
ClearObservations() when SkippedObservations() is true so that the
repeat pass does not leave skipped_observations_ set at the end of the
lifecycle update. Finally, clarify surrounding lifecycle loop comments.

Fixed: 541952000
